There are very good arguments for *not* changing orthography, the main one
that changing spelling means that you get disconnected from your past,
since reading historical sources becomes much more difficult.

For example, the "Académie Française" has kept French spelling nearly the
same for over 300 years now. That means that I can still easily read the
works of Molière, even though I'm not French myself, and my French is
therefore limited to the French that is spoken today as it is teached at
school here in the Netherlands.

British spelling might be *very* unlogical, but American English isn't
that much of an improvement. Now, if they had *really* changed it, that
would have been courageous.

